> I wish that this applied to monsters travelling staircases, too.  On
> every occasion that I've followed one on the stairs, the monster is
> on the next level immediately adjacent to the staircase, no matter
> how long I've waited to follow after him.  If it was running from me,
> shouldn't it have run as far from the staircase as possible?  This
> could be accomplished by applying a timestamp to the monster when
> it used the stairs, and looking at the current time when I follow
> it to its level, computing how far it should have travelled based
> on the time difference.

An interesting idea...
It wouldn't be just a matter of computing the distance, though:
you'd actually have to "run" the creature through N time units,
allowing it to choose its directions, run into traps, use its
wands if it so desired, etc.... and then you might run into inconsistencies
because of the fact that one monster is "living" while any others on
the levels are "frozen".  So you'd have to run the whole level simultaneously.
And then what if you had chased two or more monsters down the stairs at
different times before going down... etc.

